# LinkedIn content — Windows Network Recovery Toolkit

## Scope

Apply when drafting, editing, or reviewing files under `docs/linkedin/` (drafts, published copies, performance notes) or when generating LinkedIn posts about this repository.

Related automation (draft-only, no auto-publish): `docs/linkedin-release-automation.md` and `.github/workflows/linkedin-release-draft.yml`.

## Folder conventions

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `docs/linkedin/drafts/` | Work-in-progress posts (not yet published) |
| `docs/linkedin/published/` | Snapshot of posts that went live (for archive) |
| `docs/linkedin/performance/` | Optional engagement notes (impressions, comments) — no secrets |

Do **not** store LinkedIn tokens, Zapier webhook URLs, emails, or API keys in these folders.

## Project positioning (use real terminology)

- Technology Risk & Control Analytics / Windows endpoint **evidence** toolkit
- Evidence tiers, proof tiers, policy-gated remediation (dry-run by default)
- Audit-ready outputs and `limitations[]`
- Optional: Procmon import, proxy-watch soak, read-only monitoring dashboard, Power BI exports — only if the post is about a release that includes them

## Non-claims (never write these)

- Antivirus, EDR, XDR, malware verdicts, MITM “confirmed”
- Autonomous / silent repair of production endpoints
- Invented test counts, customers, benchmarks, or certifications
- Guaranteed security outcomes

## Tone

Professional, factual. Suitable for Technology Risk, IT Audit, Platform Engineering, SRE, and Cyber Risk audiences.

Prefer: “evidence”, “classification”, “preview”, “policy-gated”, “audit trail”.  
Avoid: “AI agent fixed your network”, “detects malware”, “enterprise-ready SLA”.

## Draft template

```text
I've released {name} of my Windows Network Recovery Toolkit.

What's new:
• {verified improvement}
• {verified improvement}
• {verified improvement}

Why it matters:
{practical impact without overclaim}

Validation:
{only verified local/CI facts}

GitHub:
{release or repo URL}

#TechnologyRisk #PlatformEngineering #SRE #Windows #OpenSource
```

## Approval before publish

- [ ] Technical claims verified against code/docs
- [ ] Test/validation lines verified (or omitted)
- [ ] No confidential host/user/path data
- [ ] Ready to publish (human posts; automation stays draft-only)